Written: `.agent/sweep/synthesis.json` — all thirteen slugs (twelve promoted threads + `mission-board`), only the five contract keys, validated against the live thread list.

What the synthesis pass actually concluded, in short:

- **Two piles.** System-building threads (extract-forge, system-audit, video-studio-edit-bay) keep finishing what they start. Revenue threads (go, proof-to-market, linkedin-launch, teardown-1-fresh, writers-room) keep stopping one step short of a buyer — a send, a post, or a decision.
- **`why` is written only where the brief will actually render a decision item** — I traced `sec_decision()` and keyed each entry to the index it builds (unfinished-line threads get index `0`; idle threads get the resume-or-park item at `0`). Threads with no open record got no `why` key rather than a filler one.
- **Two label mismatches flagged as caveats, not as facts:** `system-audit` carries a headline describing unrelated client listing work while its records are all harness maintenance; `deep-research` advertises a finished client packet while running an unrelated research lane. Both are stated as "don't trust this label," not as claims about what happened.
- **Nothing restated from the bundle.** No numbers, paths, dates, counts, or filenames — verified by regex over every string in the file (the first check tripped on `\u2014` escapes, not real digits).

I did not run `mission_brief.py build` — you asked for the JSON file only, so the briefs still carry their previous synthesis until that render runs.

Two threads where the honest read is thinner than the others: `proof-to-market` has a finished offer document and zero motion in the window, so its lede says only that; `01-listing-content` has approval recorded on the reel package but its follow-on shoot sheet was still mid-rewrite, so I split those rather than implying one approval covers both.
